@@s001 Happens-before: the rules
@@s002 dato:actions of the main,ora:t1,due:t2,no:no path
@@s003 <p>Each circle is an action of <code>PossibleReordering</code>. An arrow <code>a → b</code> means <b>a happens-before b</b>.</p><p>st1 = start(t1), jt1 = join(t1).</p>
@@s004 <p><b>Program order</b> of the main: each action of the main happens-before the next one.</p>
@@s005 <p>Program order of <b>t1</b> and of <b>t2</b>: inside each thread the order is that of the code.</p>
@@s006 <p><b>Thread start rule</b>: <code>start()</code> happens-before the <b>first</b> action of the started thread. Pairs: <code>m(start(t1)) → t1(1)</code> and <code>m(start(t2)) → t2(3)</code>.</p>
@@s007 <p><b>Thread termination rule</b>: the <b>last</b> action of a thread happens-before the <code>join()</code> on that thread. Pairs: <code>t1(2) → m(join(t1))</code> and <code>t2(4) → m(join(t2))</code>.</p>
@@s008 <p><b>Transitive rule</b>: if there is a path of arrows from a to b, then a happens-before b. For example <code>m(init(b)) → … → m(start(t1)) → t1(1) → t1(2)</code>: the initialisation of b is visible to <code>x=b</code>.</p>
@@s009 <p>And between <code>t1(1)</code> (writes a) and <code>t2(4)</code> (reads a)? Look for a path: from the actions of t1 one leaves only towards jt1, and from there one never returns to t2. And vice versa.</p><p><b>Neither pair</b> is in the order: the two actions are <b>not ordered</b> by happens-before. Accesses to the same variable, one of them a write: it is a <b>data race</b>.</p>
@@s010 dato:action,ok:pair in HB,due:pair by transitivity,no:not ordered
@@s011 dato:action a,due:action b,ok:a happens-before b
@@s012 dato:main,ora:t1,due:t2,ok:start and termination,no:missing pair
@@s013 Happens-before: the rules
@@s014 Program order orders the actions <b>inside</b> one thread. To know whether an action of one thread surely comes before an action of <b>another</b> thread (and so whether the second sees the effect of the first) the <b>happens-before</b> rules are needed.
@@s015 The pairs: what <code>a → b</code> means
@@s016 Definition
@@s017 Given two actions <code>a</code>, <code>b</code> of an execution, <b><code>a</code> happens-before <code>b</code></b> (written <code>a → b</code>) if the pair is obtained from one of the rules below. If <code>a → b</code>, the effect of <code>a</code> is <b>visible</b> to <code>b</code>.
@@s018 How the pairs are read
@@s019 Same thing, two writes: a comes before b, guaranteed.
@@s020 A chain also contains the "skipped" pairs: transitivity holds.
@@s021 If neither pair is there, the actions are <b>not ordered</b>: they can happen in any order, and neither is guaranteed to see the effect of the other.
@@s022 "Partial": not all pairs of actions are comparable. The program order of a thread, instead, is <b>total</b>.
@@s023 Pairs, transitivity, unordered actions
@@s024 The eight rules
@@s025 Happens-before is not "felt": it is <b>built</b>, and only with these eight rules. A pair (a, b) is in the set only if a rule produces it. Three rules work <b>inside</b> a thread or before everything (program order, default init, final field init). Four link <b>different threads</b> and are the only ones that allow a thread to see the writes of another (monitor, volatile, start, termination). The last, the transitive one, glues the others into chains. If between two actions of different threads you cannot build a chain with these rules, those actions are not ordered.
@@s026 Rule
@@s027 a → b when…
@@s028 Example
@@s029 <b>Program order</b>
@@s030 a and b are of the same thread and a comes first in the code
@@s031 <b>Monitor</b>
@@s032 a is an <code>unlock()</code> and b a later <code>lock()</code> on the <b>same</b> monitor (lock, semaphore, barrier…)
@@s033 <code>t1(unlock) → t2(lock)</code>
@@s034 <b>Volatile</b>
@@s035 a is a write to a volatile variable and b a later read of the same variable
@@s036 <code>m(v=42) → t(read v)</code>
@@s037 <b>Default init</b>
@@s038 the default initialisation (0, false, null) comes before any other action
@@s039 field <code>int i;</code>
@@s040 <b>Final field init</b>
@@s041 the initialisation of the <code>final</code> (and static) fields comes before any other use, once the constructor has finished
@@s042 <code>final Object o</code>
@@s043 <b>Thread start</b>
@@s044 a is <code>start()</code> and b is the <b>first</b> action of the started thread
@@s045 <code>m(start(t1)) → t1(1)</code>
@@s046 <b>Thread termination</b>
@@s047 a is the <b>last</b> action of a thread and b the <code>join()</code> on that thread, from any other thread
@@s048 <code>t1(2) → m(join(t1))</code>
@@s049 <b>Transitive</b>
@@s050 chains
@@s051 The eight rules, one at a time
@@s052 The graph, rule by rule
@@s053 Happens-before in PossibleReordering
@@s054 Question from the slide: which pairs give start and termination?
@@s055 Start rule: <code>m(start(t1)) → t1(1)</code>, <code>m(start(t2)) → t2(3)</code>. Termination rule: <code>t1(2) → m(join(t1))</code>, <code>t2(4) → m(join(t2))</code>. Careful: the start rule links <code>start</code> only to the <b>first</b> action of the thread, the termination rule only the <b>last</b>. The others follow by transitivity with the program order.
@@s056 Building the sets as in the slides
@@s057 At the exam the graph is written as <b>sets of pairs</b>: one for each rule that applies (POm, PO1, PO2 for the program order, S for start, T for termination, then possibly M for the monitor and V for volatile). The happens-before is the <b>transitive closure</b> of the union, indicated with ⁺: one adds all the pairs that follow from a chain. To decide whether two actions are ordered it is enough to look for a path in the graph, in one direction or the other.
@@s058 The sets of pairs of PossibleReordering (slide 27)
@@s059 Program order of the main.
@@s060 Program order of the threads.
@@s061 Pairs of the start rule.
@@s062 Pairs of the termination rule.
@@s063 The complete happens-before: all the pairs, plus those that follow by transitivity. Here it is the same for <b>all</b> the executions, because there is only one possible order for start and join.
@@s064 No pair links actions of t1 to actions of t2 (except going through the main in the wrong direction). Conclusion: data race between <code>t1(1)</code> and <code>t2(4)</code> in <b>every</b> execution.
@@s065 From POm, PO1, PO2, S, T to HB
